    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Research motivation</h2>

      <p>
        ADHD assessment commonly relies on behavioral observations,
        questionnaires, and clinical judgment. These sources are important,
        but they can vary across observers and settings. The study investigated
        whether physiological relationships measured during sleep could
        provide complementary, objectively derived biomarkers.
      </p>

      <p>
        Polysomnography records several interacting physiological systems
        throughout sleep. Rather than analyzing each signal independently, the
        project models relationships among EEG, EOG, EMG, and ECG channels as
        weighted networks and examines how those relationships vary across
        sleep stages.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Study cohort and polysomnography data</h2>

      <p>
        The study included 48 participants between 6 and 18 years of age:
        25 participants with ADHD and 23 control participants. Each
        participant contributed approximately eight hours of PSG recordings
        acquired through the University of Mississippi Medical Center
        Pediatric Sleep Center.
      </p>

      <p>
        The analysis included 20,294 ADHD epochs and 19,968 control epochs.
        Every 30-second epoch was sampled at 512 Hz across 17 channels:
        eight EEG channels, two EOG channels, five EMG channels, and two ECG
        channels. The recordings were organized across Wake, Sleep Stage 1,
        Sleep Stage 2, Sleep Stage 3-4, and rapid eye movement sleep.
      </p>
    </section>

