    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Graph-based feature extraction</h2>

      <p>
        For each 30-second epoch, the workflow calculates Pearson correlation
        coefficients between all pairs of the 17 physiological channels.
        Absolute correlation matrices are averaged for each participant and
        sleep stage and represented as weighted NetworkX graphs.
      </p>

      <p>
        Weighted shortest paths are calculated from each stage-specific graph,
        and their average provides one graph feature. The five sleep-stage
        features form a compact participant-level representation used by the
        machine-learning model.
      </p>

      <p>
        The public repository preserves the historical implementation exactly,
        including its use of absolute correlations directly as graph edge
        weights. It also provides a cleaned and tested Python package that
        separates data handling, graph construction, feature extraction,
        batch processing, modeling, and evaluation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Machine-learning evaluation</h2>

      <p>
        A Random Forest classifier evaluates the five-dimensional
        participant representation. The historical workflow uses an outer
        five-fold cross-validation loop for testing and an inner five-fold
        GridSearchCV loop for hyperparameter selection. The search considers
        the number of trees, tree depth, minimum split size, and minimum leaf
        size.
      </p>
    </section>

